window.docContents[59].resolve({"/Basic-Types/Strings/#string-api-pos":{"contents":"A byte position in a String, according to its UTF-8 encoding.Character positions (counting the Unicode code points rather than bytes) are represented by plain\nNats. Indexing a String by a String.Pos.Raw takes constant time, while character positions need to\nbe translated internally to byte positions, which takes linear time.A byte position p is valid for a string s if 0 ≤ p ≤ s.rawEndPos and p lies on a UTF-8\ncharacter boundary, see String.Pos.IsValid.There is another type, String.Pos, which bundles the validity predicate. Using String.Pos\ninstead of String.Pos.Raw is recommended because it will lead to less error handling and fewer edge cases.Get the underlying byte index of a String.Pos.Raw\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Basic Types\u0009Strings\u0009API Reference","header":"21.8.4.5. Raw Positions","id":"/Basic-Types/Strings/#string-api-pos"},"/The--mvcgen--tactic/Predicate-Transformers/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--The--mvcgen--tactic--Predicate-Transformers--Predicate-Transformers--Weakest-Preconditions":{"contents":"The weakest precondition semantics of a monad are provided by the WP type class.\nInstances of WP determine the monad's postcondition shape and provide the logical rules for interpreting the monad's operations as a predicate transformer in its postcondition shape.\n\nA weakest precondition interpretation of a monadic program x : m α in terms of a predicate\ntransformer PredTrans ps α. The monad m determines ps : PostShape.For practical reasoning, an instance of WPMonad m ps is typically needed in addition to WP m ps.Interpret a monadic program x : m α in terms of a predicate transformer PredTrans ps α.\n\nWeakest Preconditionswp⟦x⟧ Q is defined as (WP.wp x).apply Q.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009The  mvcgen  tactic\u0009Predicate Transformers\u0009Predicate Transformers","header":"18.2.3.1. Weakest Preconditions","id":"/The--mvcgen--tactic/Predicate-Transformers/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--The--mvcgen--tactic--Predicate-Transformers--Predicate-Transformers--Weakest-Preconditions"},"/The--mvcgen--tactic/Predicate-Transformers/#mvcgen-adequacy":{"contents":"Monads that can be invoked from pure code typically provide a invocation operator that takes any required input state as a parameter and returns either a value paired with an output state or some kind of exceptional value.\nExamples include StateT.run, ExceptT.run, and Id.run.\nAdequacy lemmas provide a bridge between statements about invocations of monadic programs and those programs' weakest precondition semantics as given by their WP instances.\nThey show that a property about the invocation is true if its weakest precondition is true.\n\nSoundness lemma for Id.run. Derived from WPSound.of_wp_canReturn: Id.run prog = x\nis the MonadAttach.CanReturn prog x witness.\n\nSoundness lemma for StateM.run: Id-specialization of StateT.of_wp_run.\n\nSoundness lemma for StateM.run': Id-specialization of StateT.of_wp_run.\n\nSoundness lemma for ReaderM.run: Id-specialization of ReaderT.of_wp_run.\n\nSoundness lemma for Except: Id-specialization of ExceptT.of_wp_run.\n\nSoundness lemma for EStateM.run.\nUseful if you want to prove a property about an expression x defined as EStateM.run prog s and\nyou want to use mvcgen to reason about prog.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009The  mvcgen  tactic\u0009Predicate Transformers\u0009Predicate Transformers","header":"18.2.3.3. Adequacy Lemmas","id":"/The--mvcgen--tactic/Predicate-Transformers/#mvcgen-adequacy"},"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-rc2-_LPAR_2026-10-27_RPAR_--Library":{"contents":"* #15654\nfixes a memory leak in String.toByteArray and String.toUTF8 that has been present for a long time but was uncovered by a recently added test.* #15581\nredefines the order on String to be the lexicographic order of the UTF-8 encoding, not of the character list.* #15659\nexports the lemmas declared in Init.Data.Int.DivMod.Pow.* #15651\nadds String.fromUTF8Lossy, a function that attempts to parse a ByteArray into a String, replacing invalid UTF-8 with the Unicode replacement character in a certain well-defined way.* #15647\nadds missing simprocs for bitgwise operations on UIntX/IntX.* #15628\nmakes Lean.Json.parse reject JSON objects that contain the same key twice (compared after unescaping), instead of silently keeping the last value.* #15594\nrenames error function names and other helper functions.* #15547\nadd RAII-based scoped lock guard* #15545\nremoves the allocation of the libuv handle in uv sockets, timer and signal objects.* #15571\nfixes how 0 is handled in the LT JsonNumber instance. Before the fix, (0 : JsonNumber) < 5e-2 would evaluate to false.* #15523\nfixes crashes in libuv timers and signals:* One shot timer and signal callbacks resolved their promise before releasing the event loop's reference to the handle (sync := true) caused this.* libuv treats a repeat period of 0 as a oneshot timer, so a repeating Timer with a 0 ms period fired once and every later promise stayed pending. It now ticks every millisecond.* Signal.cancel only drops the pending promise and keeps the handler listening, and a signal that arrives with no promise pending resolves the promise of the next next.* Signal waiters resolve with the number from Signal.toInt32 on every platform, instead of the operating system's number for that signal.* #15469\nmakes the Std.Http server generate its Date header in the HTTP-date format of RFC 9110 §5.6.7, for example Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT, instead of with a numeric offset (+0000). Clients that parse HTTP dates strictly, such as Apache HttpClient's cache, Go's http.ParseTime and Rust's httpdate, did not accept the previous form.* #15533\nreimplements String.toList in Lean. In doing so it also removes all hints that\nString.data ever existed as it has now almost been a year since its deprecation.* #15443\nmakes the Std.Http server send responses on systems without a timezone database (for example the ubuntu:24.04 Docker image, which does not ship tzdata). Previously the server closed every connection without a response, because generating the Date header looked up UTC in the zoneinfo files.* #15517\nchanges the index of Lean.Order.iSup and Lean.Order.iInf to {ι : Sort v}, so ⨆ h : P, f h over a proposition P elaborates. The lemmas le_iSup, iSup_le, iInf_le, le_iInf, iSup_apply and iInf_apply take the same Sort index.* #15175\nstops Loop.configure from leaking and rejects System.random values above 2^31-1 before allocating a huge array.* #15329\nadds efficient LE and Ord instances for ByteArray and deduces all other order instances from that.* #15316\nremoves the duplicate BEq (Option a) instance derived in Init.Data.Option.Basic and instead makes the one derived in Init.Core exposed.* #15315\nchanges the attributes at Array.compareLex and Vector.compareLex to make sure that they actually get specialized. Previously, the comparison function was just passed by closure.* #15304\nadds the missing order instances for List, Array and Vector.* #15299\nmakes grind, and with it vcgen … with finish, prove goals about assertions that use lattice operations more reliably. For example, the nodup proof of the vcgen tutorial now closes with vcgen … with finish, as it used to with mvgen.* #15414\ntunes down some overeager grind e-matching annotations that would over-eagerly trigger cross-theory reasoning.* #15387\nadds lemmas to the lattice theory in Std.Internal.Order and computation rules for predicate transformers.* #15192\nfixes ContextAsync.race and ContextAsync.raceAll,ContextAsync.run and ContextAsync.background cancellations and Notify.notifyOne no longer spends its wakeup on a waiter whose select was already decided.* #15274\nadds the missing order instances on BitVec.* #15174\nsolves multiple issues in the TCP and UDP modules.* #15266\nadds logical models for the Float.minimum and Float.minimumNumber operations that were added in #15257.* #15257\ndefines Float.minimum and Float.minimumNumber, which correspond to the IEEE-754-2019 operations of the same name. It redefines Min Float to mean Float.minimum.* #15115\nexposes Char.ordinal, Char.ofOrdinal, Char.succ? and Char.succMany?, so that they reduce across module boundaries. Ranges over Char are built from Char.succ? and Char.succMany? via the UpwardEnumerable Char instance, so previously cbv and kernel reduction got stuck on these in any file using the module system.* #15247\nintroduces a basic FFI module for CaDiCaL as well as a small wrapper around it to interact with our Std.Sat API.* #15252\nremoves the withPtrEq function.* #15217\nrenames the EPred type variable to EPosts and the epost value variable to eposts, since they denote a bundle of exception postconditions rather than a single predicate, and adds deprecated_arg aliases so code passing EPred by name keeps compiling.* #15171\nrenames several Std.WP names for consistency and deprecates the old ones. The WP.wpTrans projection becomes WP.trans, its monotonicity field WP.wp_trans_monotone becomes WP.trans_monotone, and the wpTrans_apply_eq lemma becomes trans_apply_eq. The weakest-precondition monotonicity lemmas WP.wp_consequence/wp_econs become a uniform wp_monotone family: wp_monotone for both channels, wp_monotone_post and wp_monotone_epost for one channel, wp_monotone_bot for the empty exception postcondition, and the _le transitivity variants.* #15201\nrefactors the AIG to CNF transformation from only acting on AIG Nat to acting on generic AIG types. This has two advantages:1. We don't have to perform the relabelNat step anymore and the CNF representation gets slightly more efficient. This is because instead of using the Nat from the AIG atom as the identifier we now use the indices into the AIG as an identifier and thus avoid producing one tseitin atom per AIG atom.2. In the future we will be able to use the AIG to CNF API in an incremental fashion across multiple runs. Towards this we also already introduce the State.cast API for reusing a cache state across runs.* #15067\nextends the frame mechanism to the exception channel of Std.WP. For example, a program logic whose exceptional exits carry state now supports a frame rule such as ⦃P⦄ x ⦃Q; E⦄ → ⦃F ⋆ P⦄ x ⦃F ⋆ Q; F ⋆ E⦄. The frame closure, the frame condition, and the frame rule gain a companion operator opE : R → EPred → EPred for the exception postcondition that defaults to ignoring the frame.* #14793\nfixes #14776* #14795\nfixes two issues in recv functions of the UDP and TCP modules.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.36.0-rc2 (2026-10-27)","header":"Library","id":"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-rc2-_LPAR_2026-10-27_RPAR_--Library"}});